 Discrete event simulation (DES) is a modeling paradigm in which system state changes occur at discrete points in time triggered by events, and the simulation clock advances from event to event rather than in fixed increments \citep{banks2010discrete}. DES is the dominant paradigm in manufacturing, logistics, healthcare, and queueing systems, where entities (customers, parts, patients) move through networks of resources (machines, servers, beds) via process-based interactions.
 
-Despite its natural fit for modeling vehicles moving through infrastructure, DES has seen limited adoption in traffic simulation. Notable exceptions include Burghout's hybrid mesoscopic-microscopic framework \citep{burghout2005hybrid}, which uses DES at the mesoscopic level to model link traversal times while delegating vehicle interactions to a microscopic sub-model. Several studies have explored event-based traffic signal control \citep{barcelo2010models}, and queueing-network representations of signalized intersections naturally lend themselves to DES \citep{osorio2013simulation}. However, these approaches typically model vehicles at an aggregate or mesoscopic level, without the individual vehicle trajectory resolution needed for lane-level interactions, car-following dynamics, and mixed-autonomy analysis. To the authors' knowledge, no prior work has combined a cell-based spatial structure (as in CA models) with a process-based DES engine to model individual vehicle movements as resource acquisition events.
+Despite its natural fit for modeling vehicles moving through infrastructure, DES has seen limited adoption in \emph{microscopic} traffic simulation, where the fixed-timestep paradigm remains dominant. Burghout's hybrid mesoscopic-microscopic framework \citep{burghout2005hybrid} uses DES at the mesoscopic level to model link traversal times while delegating vehicle interactions to a microscopic sub-model. Several studies have explored event-based traffic signal control \citep{barcelo2010models}, and queueing-network representations of signalized intersections naturally lend themselves to DES \citep{osorio2013simulation}. These approaches model vehicles at an aggregate or mesoscopic level, without the individual trajectory resolution needed for lane-level interactions, car-following dynamics, and mixed-autonomy analysis.
 
-The present framework leverages the DES paradigm through SimPy, a process-based DES library for Python \citep{simpy2023}. In this paradigm, each vehicle is a generator process that can request resources, wait for availability, and proceed upon acquisition, a natural mapping to the request--seize--delay--release cycle of cell-by-cell vehicle movement.
+The closest antecedent of the present work is the Cell-DEVS formalism \citep{wainer2001timed,wainer2014cellular}, a discrete-event specification of cellular automata built on Zeigler's DEVS \citep{zeigler2000theory}. Cell-DEVS makes each cell an atomic DEVS model whose state changes propagate through the cell space via transport or inertial delays, so cell updates are asynchronous and event-driven rather than synchronous, and it has been applied to traffic directly: \citet{davidson2000specifying} specify truck movement as Cell-DEVS rules, and the ATLAS language \citep{wainer2006atlas} compiles city sections into Cell-DEVS cell spaces for microsimulation. Combining a cell-based spatial structure with a discrete-event engine is therefore established ground, and the present work does not claim it.
+
+ODCA-DES therefore shares with Cell-DEVS the rejection of the synchronous update, and departs from it on three points, of which the first entails the other two.
+
+\emph{What is active.} In Cell-DEVS the cell is the atomic model: it holds state, owns a transition function, and a vehicle exists as a value that propagates from one cell's state to the next. In ODCA-DES the vehicle is the process and the cell is a resource of capacity one with no behavior of its own. Behavior is attached to the object that has it in the real system, which is why a heterogeneous fleet costs nothing structurally here: a driver model is a property of a vehicle process, not a case in a rule that every cell in the lattice must evaluate.
+
+\emph{What a speed is.} A Cell-DEVS traffic cell still encodes speed in its discrete state, so the integer-speed artifact of classical CA survives the move to discrete events. In ODCA-DES speed is a real number carried by the vehicle, and the cell crossing time $\ell/v$ is whatever that number makes it, so the artifact does not survive (Section~\ref{sec:speed_representation}).
+
+\emph{Where congestion comes from.} Cell-DEVS expresses interaction as cell rules over a neighborhood, in the CA tradition. ODCA-DES expresses it as contention for a resource: a vehicle requesting an occupied cell waits, and the delayed release of Section~\ref{sec:headway} makes that wait reproduce Newell's headway analytically rather than by rule calibration. This is the step that yields the closed-form capacity of Section~\ref{sec:fd_derivation}, which has no counterpart in the Cell-DEVS traffic models.
+
+The claim of this paper is therefore narrower than a new use of discrete events in traffic, and more specific: to the authors' knowledge, no prior work models individual vehicle movement as resource acquisition on a cell lattice, and none derives headway and capacity in closed form from the acquisition protocol itself.
+
+The present framework adopts the DES paradigm through SimPy, a process-based DES library for Python \citep{simpy2023}. In this paradigm, each vehicle is a generator process that can request resources, wait for availability, and proceed upon acquisition, a natural mapping to the request--seize--delay--release cycle of cell-by-cell vehicle movement.
 
