 \paragraph{Throughput.} Network throughput increases with AV penetration, from $5{,}874 \pm 19$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 0\%$ to $6{,}983 \pm 34$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 70\%$, an 18.9\% improvement, and the confidence intervals are tight (coefficient of variation 0.7--1.1\%), so 20 replications resolve the differences comfortably. Most of the gain is already realised at 30\% AV ($6{,}679 \pm 25$, that is 13.7\%), and the last two scenarios are within 0.5\% of each other. The reason is that the network runs out of demand before it runs out of capacity: mainline and ramps offer 7{,}000\,veh/h, so $6{,}983$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 70\%$ is 99.8\% of everything that asks to enter. The all-HDV baseline, by contrast, serves 83.9\% of the offered demand and holds the rest back in the origin queues. The measured improvement is therefore a lower bound on the capacity effect: the theoretical 71\% capacity increase (Table~\ref{tab:theoretical_capacity}) cannot show up in a throughput figure that the demand caps. Where the extra capacity does show up is in delay, which keeps falling after throughput has saturated. Figure~\ref{fig:throughput_bar} visualizes the throughput and delay trends with 95\% confidence intervals.
 
